Accession Number   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status  FinalType  Surgical Pathology Pathology Report   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  OPERATION DATE  24 REMOVED_MONTH 03 ACCESSIONED ON  24 REMOVED_MONTH 03 at 15 50 CLINICAL DATA  59 year old Asian female  status post ultrasound guided core biopsy   right breast ovoid hypoechoic solid mass at 9 o clock  REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 1 0cm    fibroadenoma versus carcinoma                                                                                      FINAL DIAGNOSIS     BREAST  RIGHT   CORE BIOPSIES  DIFFUSE LARGE B CELL LYMPHOMA  NOTE  Sections show a dense diffuse infiltrate of medium sized and large atypical lymphoid cells with oval or slightly irregular nuclei  prominent nucleoli and scant cytoplasm  Mitoses are frequent  Occasional tingible body macrophages are present  The histologic features are consistent with lymphoma and immunostains support the diagnosis   See addendum   Diagnosis by  Judith Ann Ferry  M D  Signed On  30 REMOVED_MONTH 03 at 15 07                                                                                     IMMUNOHISTOCHEMISTRY Addendum     RESULTS OF IMMUNOPEROXIDASE STAINS  PARAFFIN SECTIONS With antibodies to immunoglobulin light chains  kappa  lambda  there is monotypic cytoplasm  Ig kappa  staining of many lymphoid cells in a diffuse pattern  With antibodies to B lineage antigen s    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   there is staining of most cells in a diffuse pattern  The B cells are negative for antibody to  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  and  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   With antibodies to T lineage associated antigen s    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   there is staining of rare small cells scattered within the tissue  With antibody to cytokeratin   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  3 cam 5 2  there is no staining of tumor cells  The cells are also positive for bcl 2 and many are positive for bcl 6  Overall  approximately 70  of the tumor cells stain for Ki 67  proliferation   Together with the morphologic features on routine sections  these results support the diagnosis of diffuse large B cell lymphoma  See note  NOTE  These tests were developed and their performance characteristics determined by the Pathology Department at the Massachusetts General Hospital  They have not been cleared or approved by the U S  Food and Drug Administration  FDA   The FDA has determined that such clearance or approval is not necessary  this laboratory has established and verified the test s accuracy and precision  Added by  Judith Ann Ferry  M D  Signed On  31 REMOVED_MONTH 03 at 13 35 GROSS DESCRIPTION  Received in formalin   labeled REMOVE_PATIENT_NAME  patient s name  unit number and  right breast   are multiple cores of fibrofatty tissue ranging from REMOVED_DATE x 0 2cm to REMOVED_DATE x 0 2cm which are submitted in toto as cassette A and B  Gross Description by  JULIE ZWIRBLE Resident  MINDY J  HULL  MD                                                                                 SOURCE CARE UNIT  MGH OUTPATIENT  OFF P CLINIC  REPORTS TO  Diane R Fingold  M D  Helenanne D alessandro  M D  John Henry Eichhorn  M D  Lan Wang  M D  PART   BREAST  RIGHT   CORE BIOPSIES , Unchecked.
Accession Number   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status  Corrected Type  Surgical Pathology Pathology Report   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  OPERATION DATE  29 REMOVED_MONTH 96 ACCESSIONED ON  29 REMOVED_MONTH 96 at 12 29 CLINICAL DATA   None Provided                                                                                      FINAL DIAGNOSIS     BREAST  RIGHT   SIMPLE MASTECTOMY  INVASIVE DUCTAL CARCINOMA  GRADE 2  REMOVED_DATE CM INVADING LYMPHATIC VESSELS DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU  RADIATION CHANGES  SEE NOTE  NOTE  Blood vessel invasion is not seen  The margins of the specimen appear free of the tumor  Diagnosis by  Frederick Carl Koerner  M D  Signed On  06 REMOVED_MONTH 96 at 14 55                                                                                     RECEPTOR IMMUNOHISTOCHEMISTRY Addendum     RESULTS OF STAINING OF FIXED TISSUE FOR HORMONE RECEPTOR PROTEINS BREAST  RIGHT   THE TUMOR CELLS STAIN FOR ESTROGEN RECEPTOR PROTEIN  THE TUMOR CELLS STAIN FOR PROGESTERONE RECEPTOR PROTEIN  SEE NOTE  Note  In this laboratory  we used this antibody to stain 100 breast carcinomas prepared in the usual way  paraffin sections  and we compared the results with determinations of estrogen receptor by biochemical assay  91  of tumors that stained with this antibody were positive for estrogen receptors by biochemical assay  96  of tumors that failed to stain with this antibody were negative for estrogen receptors by biochemical assay  When we compared the results of staining of paraffin sections with the results of staining of frozen sections  the predictive value of a positive result was 97  and the predictive value of a negative result was 95   In this laboratory  we also compared the results of staining of 100 routinely processed breast carcinomas with determinations of progesterone receptors by a hormone binding assay  Sixty five percent of tumors that stained with this antibody contained progesterone receptors by the biochemical assay  and 98  of tumors that failed to stain lacked the receptors  Added by  Frederick Carl Koerner  M D  Signed On  14 REMOVED_MONTH 96 at 08 56     RECEPTOR IMMUNOHISTOCHEMISTRY Addendum     THIS ADDENDUM HAS BEEN DELETED FOR THIS REPORT GROSS DESCRIPTION  Received fresh   labeled REMOVE_PATIENT_NAME  patient s name  unit number  and  Right breast  is a right total mastectomy specimen measuring REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 3 0cm with an attached irregularly shaped fragment of skin measuring in maximum dimension REMOVED_DATE x 4 2cm  A grossly unremarkable nipple is present on the skin  Sectioning reveals a white firm area measuring REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 1 0cm  The lesion grossly approaches the deep resection margin to 0 4cm and the inferior margin which is the closest resection margin to less than 0 1cm  Section Code  A B  lesion and inferior margin  C D  lesion and deep margin  E  lesion with overlying skin  F K  representative sections of the lesion  Resident  ALEXANDER KRATZ  MD                                                                                 SOURCE CARE UNIT  MGH INPATIENT  FLR UNKNOWN  REPORTS TO  Michele Annette Gadd  M D  W B Jerry Younger  M D  PART   RIGHT SIMPLE MASTECTOMY   report_end  , Unchecked.
Accession Number   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status  Final Type  Surgical Pathology Pathology Report   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  OPERATION DATE  29 AUG 94 ACCESSIONED ON  29 AUG 94 at 11 42 CLINICAL DATA   None Provided                                                                                      FINAL DIAGNOSIS     BREAST  RIGHT   BIOPSY  MUCINOUS  COLLOID   CARCINOMA  SEE NOTE   NOTE  The tumor measures 6 0mm in greatest dimension and is composed entirely of mucinous carcinoma  Tumor is present at an inked resection margin  Diagnosis by  Austin L  Vickery  M D  Signed On  31 AUG 94 at 15 19                                                                                     RECEPTOR IMMUNOHISTOCHEMISTRY Addendum     RESULTS OF STAINING OF FIXED TISSUE FOR HORMONE RECEPTOR PROTEINS BREAST  RIGHT   THE TUMOR CELLS STAIN FOR ESTROGEN RECEPTOR PROTEIN  THE TUMOR CELLS STAIN FOR PROGESTERONE RECEPTOR PROTEIN  SEE NOTE  Note  In this laboratory  we used this antibody to stain 100 breast carcinomas prepared in the usual way  paraffin sections  and we compared the results with determinations of estrogen receptor by biochemical assay  91  of tumors that stained with this antibody were positive for estrogen receptors by biochemical assay  96  of tumors that failed to stain with this antibody were negative for estrogen receptors by biochemical assay  When we compared the results of staining of paraffin sections with the results of staining of frozen sections  the predictive value of a positive result was 97  and the predictive value of a negative result was 95   In this laboratory  we also compared the results of staining of 100 routinely processed breast carcinomas with determinations of progesterone receptors by a hormone binding assay  Sixty five percent of tumors that stained with this antibody contained progesterone receptors by the biochemical assay  and 98  of tumors that failed to stain lacked the receptors  THE RESULTS OF IMMUNOHISTOCHEMICAL STAINING FOR PROGES